            <ArticleTitle>Effect of lumen narrowing within coronary stents on proximal and distal vessel segments following bare metal stent implantation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>376-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Adjacent reference vessel response to smaller lumens at stented segments was examined with 3-dimensional intravascular ultrasound analysis. In 128 patients after bare metal stent implantation, minimal lumen area (MLA) within the stent and average lumen area at distal/proximal adjacent reference segments (5 mm) were obtained at baseline and follow-up. In the smaller in-stent MLA group (MLA &lt;3 mm2), lumen area decreased significantly at the distal edge compared with the larger in-stent MLA group (MLA &gt; or =3 mm2), although no significant difference was seen at the proximal edge. In-stent lumen patency may influence vascular responses at adjacent reference segments after bare metal stent implantation.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
